{
"slug": "periodic-trends-pes",
"kind": "simulator",
"topic": "periodic-trends",
"about": "Trainer: periodic trends and photoelectron spectra (Unit 1). Explore: a periodic table colored by a trend; tap an element for its PES and configuration. Test yourself: seeded predict-then-reveal problems from ApChemMath.trends.generate, rechecked by scripts/lib/apchem-tool-checks/periodic-trends-pes.mjs.",
"sources": {
"radius": "Atomic radius in pm. Metals: metallic radius (12-coordinate), as tabulated in Greenwood and Earnshaw, Chemistry of the Elements (1997) and Slater, J. Chem. Phys. 41, 3199 (1964), via the Wikipedia 'Atomic radii of the elements (data page)'. Nonmetals and metalloids: single-bond covalent radius, Pyykko and Atsumi, Chem. Eur. J. 15, 186 (2009). Noble gases: no comparable bonded radius, so none is shown. rk: m metallic, c covalent.",
"ie": "First ionization energy in kJ/mol: NIST Atomic Spectra Database ground-state ionization energies (eV) x 96.4853.",
"en": "Pauling electronegativity: the same values as the site's periodic table popup (ochem/assets/periodic-table.js; CRC Handbook).",
"ea": "Electron affinity in kJ/mol released when the gaseous atom gains an electron: Wikipedia 'Electron affinity (data page)' (from Andersen, Phys. Rep. 394, 157 (2004); Rienstra-Kiracofe et al., Chem. Rev. 102, 231 (2002); CRC). Missing: no stable anion (estimated value below zero). eaEst: estimated or calculated.",
"conf": "Ground-state configurations: NIST ASD, through the mendeleev package (v1.3).",
"pes": "Photoelectron spectra, binding energy in MJ/mol per subshell [subshell, energy, electrons], for free (gas-phase) atoms. H to Ca: the table used in AP chemistry teaching materials (e.g. Flinn Scientific PES activity), which matches Carlson, Photoelectron and Auger Spectroscopy (1976) and Lotz, J. Opt. Soc. Am. 60, 206 (1970) for the free atoms; Sc to Kr: LLNL Evaluated Atomic Data Library, Perkins et al., UCRL-50400 Vol. 30 (1991), spin-orbit pairs averaged (Cr and Cu 3d from Carlson). The lowest peak of every atom is its NIST first ionization energy."
},
"intro": "<p>Every periodic trend comes from Coulomb's law: how many protons pull on the outer electrons, how far away those electrons are, and how many inner electrons shield them. Color the table by a trend, then tap an element to see its photoelectron spectrum, the experiment that shows its electron configuration.</p>",
"howItWorks": "<p class=\"bt-eq\">Attraction ∝ (charge of nucleus × charge of electron) / distance²</p><ul><li><b>Across a period</b>: one more proton each step, the same inner shells. The effective nuclear charge (about protons − core electrons) rises, so radius shrinks and ionization energy and electronegativity rise.</li><li><b>Down a group</b>: one more occupied shell. The outer electrons are farther away and better shielded, so radius grows and ionization energy and electronegativity fall.</li><li><b>Photoelectron spectrum</b>: one peak per subshell; the position is the binding energy (how tightly the nucleus holds those electrons), the height is the number of electrons.</li></ul><p>The numbers are measured values (sources in the tool's data file). Radii of metals are metallic radii and of nonmetals covalent radii; noble gases have no comparable radius. Spectra are for gas-phase atoms, drawn on a stretched (logarithmic) energy axis so the 1s peak fits.</p>",
"partTopics": {
"pes": "photoelectron-spectroscopy",
"config": "electron-configuration"
},
